  "step_by_step_reasoning": "I am currently on a search results page for the article 'One in three using AI for emotional support and conversation, UK says'. The accessibility tree shows an article with a heading containing a link to the article at URL '/?p=487'. This is the target article I need to open. I can click this link to navigate to the article page. Once on the article page, I need to check if it contains an image related to 'Getty Images'. To do this, I can use the knowledge base function 'extract_images_from_page_manual' to extract all images from the article page. This function is suitable because it avoids navigation timeouts by using 'load' state and extracts image metadata including src and alt attributes. After extracting the images, I will check if any image's src or alt contains 'Getty Images' (case-insensitive). I must return the result as a direct response to the task query, which is a boolean indicating whether such an image exists. I will not process the data beyond this check, as per instructions. Steps: 1. Click the link to the article. 2. Wait for the article page to load. 3. Call 'extract_images_from_page_manual' with the relative URL of the article page. 4. Check the images for 'Getty Images'. 5. Return the result as a string (e.g., 'True' or 'False').",
  "python_code": "async def act(page):\n    # Step 1: Click the link to the article from the search results\n    article_link = page.get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"One in three using AI for emotional support and conversation, UK says\").get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"One in three using AI for emotional support and conversation, UK says\")\n    await article_link.click()\n    \n    # Step 2: Wait for the article page to load\n    await page.wait_for_load_state(\"load\")\n    \n    # Step 3: Extract images from the article page using the knowledge base function\n    # The current URL is the article page, get the relative part\n    current_url = page.url\n    relative_url = current_url.replace(\"http://localhost:8000\", \"\")\n    image_data = await extract_images_from_page_manual(page, relative_url)\n    \n    # Step 4: Check if any image is related to 'Getty Images'\n    contains_getty_image = False\n    for image in image_data['images']:\n        src = image.get('src', '')\n        alt = image.get('alt', '')\n        if src and 'Getty Images' in src:\n            contains_getty_image = True\n            break\n        if alt and 'Getty Images' in alt:\n            contains_getty_image = True\n            break\n    \n    # Step 5: Return the result as a string\n    return str(contains_getty_image)",
